How to start a new species dashboard. 
October 2026

Set up -------------------------------------------

1) In the Draft_Species_Summaries folder in CCISS_ShinyApp, make a copy of the Template_Summary_Dash_3tab.qmd
  - Rename it with the species abbreviation

2) Create a new referencesXX.bib file (using the species abbreviation in place of the "XX")

3) In the qmd file, follow the instructions at the top to find and replace all YYYY = Full name or XX = sp code. 


Filling in species-specific fields ------------------------------------------

Following are the sections that need to be filled in: 


Summary info for XX:
1) find the NRCan tree outline online and add it to the Figures/"Sp" folder (.gif or .png)
    Note: the .gif version of the figure is sized correctly. If you use the .png version, you might need to adjust the sizing with "#| out.width: "75%""
    Temporary note: The NRCan website is currently down, so you might not be able to find and download the tree image until they come back online.
    Note: add the tree species scientific name to the figure caption.

2) Fill in the "High level species summary text"


Reference period page:
1) Species geographic and climatic range
Note: Use information from the BC gov species compendium pages, NRCan species summaries, and/or other references. 

2) Map captions
    -	C4
    -	B2
    -	D6
Note: Add "Remaining areas (in white) are unsuitable for Xx under historic climates on medium/mesic sites." to the end of the historical suitability descriptions.
    
3)	BGC text


CCISS projections:
1) Broad trends text (describe bubble plot)

2) Suitability changes (map) summary text

3) Shifts in suitable area (Alluvial plot) summary text


Forest Health:
1) Biotic factors

2) Climate change and drought (you can change this section title as appropriate for each species)

3) Any other sections needed?


Adding references ----------------------------------
As you fill in the text, you add citations using [@tagname]. For these to show up correctly in the rendered draft, every citation must be included in the .bib file, and the .bib file must be saved.

So far, we have different approaches that work to fill out the .bib file. You can export references from zotero, or manually enter them to your .bib file as you write. AI is helpful here, for converting citations into .bib format if you're not familiar with it. Entries should look like:

@article{mahony_global_2022,
	title = {A global climate model ensemble for downscaled monthly climate normals over {North} {America}},
	volume = {42},
	doi = {10.1002/joc.7566},
	number = {11},
	journal = {International Journal of Climatology},
	author = {Mahony, Colin R. and Wang, Tongli and Hamann, Andreas and Cannon, Alex J.},
	year = {2022},
	pages = {5871--5891},
}

And to then use that reference in the .qmd dashboard, you would cite it like [@mahony_global_2022].

I suggest pulling in the freqently-used citations from pre-existing .bibs of other species when you first creat the file. 
(the Mahony et al. 2022 citation, example above, is cited in the figure captions, copy into each ReferencesXx.bib file)

All references cited in the dashboard show up automatically in the References tab.


Rendering and pushing to git -----------------------------------------

As you're working, you can hit Render and it will locally render it in a tab in your browser.

When you're finished, you push the .qmd file, .bib file, and the tree outline image in the Figures folder. DO NOT push the .html file that generates locally on your device when you render the dashboard. It is not needed.



Other general writing notes -------------------------------------------

- On each individual tab (e.g. Reference period, CCISS projections), the first time the species is mentioned, use "common name (Xx)"" e.g. "ponderosa pine (Py)", for the rest of the page refer to the species using the abbreviation (e.g. "Py").
- BGC zone names are capitalized (e.g. Engelmann Spruce--Subalpine Fir)
- Common tree species names are lower case (e.g. western redcedar), unless it contains a proper noun (e.g. Douglas-fir)
